What to Include in the System Design Section of Your Capstone
1. System Overview / Architecture Summary
A high-level description of what the system does and how the components interact.

Include:

Purpose of the system (connect skilled workers with clients)

Target users (carpenters, plumbers, hairdressers, clients in Douala)

High-level architecture (client–server model, web app, database)

A simple diagram helps:

User Interface (web browser)

Backend server (API, business logic)

Database (workers, services, reviews)

Optional: Notification service, authentication service

⭐ 2. Functional Requirements
These describe what the system should do.

Examples for your project:

User registration (workers and clients)

Worker profile creation (skills, location, portfolio, pricing)

Search and filtering (by skill, location, rating)

Booking or contact system

Review and rating system

Admin dashboard (approve workers, manage content)

⭐ 3. Non‑Functional Requirements
These describe how the system should behave.

Examples:

Performance: Fast search results

Scalability: Ability to add more cities beyond Douala

Security: Password hashing, secure login

Usability: Simple UI for low‑tech users

Availability: System uptime expectations

Localization: Support for French and English (Cameroon context)

⭐ 4. System Architecture Design
This is where you show the technical structure.

Include:

Architecture style:

3‑tier architecture (presentation, logic, data)

or MVC (Model–View–Controller)

Technology stack (example):

Frontend: React, Vue, or plain HTML/CSS/JS

Backend: Node.js, Django, Laravel

Database: MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB

Hosting: AWS, Azure, or local server

API design (REST endpoints)

/workers

/services

/reviews

/auth/login

⭐ 5. Database Design
This is essential.

Include:

ER Diagram (Entity–Relationship Diagram)

Tables and attributes

Example tables:

Workers (id, name, skill, location, phone, photo)

Services (id, worker_id, description, price)

Users (id, name, email, password)

Reviews (id, worker_id, user_id, rating, comment)

Bookings (optional)

Explain relationships:

One worker → many services

One worker → many reviews

One user → many bookings

⭐ 6. User Interface (UI) / UX Design
Show how the system will look and feel.

Include:

Wireframes or mockups

User flow diagrams (e.g., how a client finds a carpenter)

Accessibility considerations (simple UI for low‑literacy users)

⭐ 7. System Workflow Diagrams
These help explain how processes work.

Useful diagrams:

Use Case Diagram (workers register, clients search, admin approves)

Sequence Diagram (client searches → backend → database → results)

Activity Diagram (booking flow)

⭐ 8. Security Design
Important for any modern system.

Include:

Authentication (JWT, sessions)

Password hashing

Input validation

Role-based access (admin vs worker vs client)

Data privacy considerations

⭐ 9. Deployment Design
Explain how the system will be deployed.

Include:

Hosting environment

CI/CD pipeline (optional)

Backup strategy

Domain and SSL certificate

⭐ 10. Constraints and Assumptions
This shows you understand the real-world context.

Examples:

Many workers may not have smartphones → system must be mobile-friendly

Internet connectivity in Douala varies → lightweight pages

Workers may prefer WhatsApp → integrate WhatsApp contact button

⭐ 11. Future Enhancements
Show that your system can grow.

Examples:

Mobile app version

Payment integration (MTN Mobile Money, Orange Money)

AI-based worker recommendation

Expansion to other cities (Yaoundé, Buea)

